#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The gate: the floor every change runs, plus the tests the lane chooses.

    tools/gate --suggest                          # what the change can affect, read statically (no Buck2)
    tools/gate --tests <test>... --why "<line>"   # the floor and the chosen tests
    tools/gate --why "<line>"                     # the floor alone
    tools/gate --extend [--tests ...] --why ...   # after an edit to a passed change
    tools/gate --base <commit> ...                # the change from <commit> (for example a replay)
    tools/gate --full                             # every stage, on demand at milestone cuts
    tools/gate --bless-perf-counts                 # regenerate the reviewed instruction budgets
    tools/gate --status                           # the slot queues: holders, their age, your position
    tools/gate --set-slots N                      # change the normal pool's cap
    tools/gate --set-express-slots N              # change the express pool's cap

The floor runs on every gate: documentation links and whitespace, the gate
tooling's tests when it changed (the Lean model's checks when it or its
vectors changed), formatting, Clippy, the workspace build and the build-file
check. They run before the gate queues for a slot, and a failure stops it
there: a red floor never waits in the queue. Then, holding a slot, the test
targets of every crate whose source changed and of every changed test file,
and every changed or added corpus case with its twins. The chosen corpus
cases run first, in their own invocation; when one fails, the gate stops
before the long targets (`--extend` after the fix reuses what passed).

A gate restarted on the same change identity, or extended, keeps the queue
place of this worktree's unfinished gate (the time it first queued). Failures
listed in origin/main's tools/known-failures.tsv (test id, reason, owner lane,
date) are excluded, and each exclusion is printed with its row. Every gate
appends its floor time, queue wait, time to its first failure and total to
`hirsel-gates.jsonl` in the clone's common git directory.

`--tests` adds what the lane chooses, in one Buck2 invocation with the floor:

    <test target>          a Buck2 test, `//:name` or `name`; a sharded binary's name runs every shard
    conformance            the whole conformance campaign (16 shards)
    agreement              the whole source/IR agreement sample (seed 1, 16 shards)
    native-smoke           the native smoke, toolchain proof and two-host link gate
    conformance:<path>     the corpus cases under <path> (a case, or a directory prefix)
    agreement:<path>       those cases' variants in the seed-1 sample
    native:<program>       the LLVM smoke matrix and Cranelift parity for <program>
    all-tests              every Buck2 test
    suggested              what --suggest prints (tools/buck2/impact.py)

`--why` says in one line why that selection suffices. The green record keeps
the selection, the floor and the reason, and tools/land prints them, so a red
main maps back to the push and to what it chose to skip. Nothing runs the full
suite on a schedule: per-push selection and its floor are the routine net, and
`tools/gate --full` runs on demand at milestone cuts.

`--extend` adds an edit to a change that already passed: it runs the floor of
the paths whose change differs from the last recorded pass, the test targets
that own them and any `--tests`, and records the edited change's identity.
For an identical change whose gate failed, it resumes that gate's selection,
rerunning local checks and only Buck2 targets without a recorded pass. Target
results are reusable only with the same change identity and campaign settings;
a partial receipt never authorizes tools/land.

Gates share the host's slot directory, /workspace/code/hirsel-briefs/locks/gate-slots
(`$HIRSEL_GATE_SLOTS` names another, or `none` for no cap; a `:N` suffix is
ignored). Its `count` file caps normal gates (6 when absent): the count is the
host's, never a process's. A settled selection with
no whole campaign or native programs and at most four distinct test targets
(including selected corpus shards) uses a separate express pool: its
`express-count` defaults to 2. Four accommodates one conformance slice (up to
20 cases), one agreement slice (up to 260 cases), and two other test targets.
Each pool has its own FIFO, and re-reads its count every poll. `--set-slots N`
and `--set-express-slots N` atomically update their counts without running a
gate; neither selects a gate's class. Lowering a count leaves held slots
running. Output and the green receipt name the pool;
tooling-only gates use no pool. `--status` prints each pool's cap, its holders
and how long they have held, the queue in order, and this worktree's place.

A passing change is remembered by its identity (gating.change_identity: the
patch-id of the lane's own diff without the generated docs/api.md, and the
crates it touches). When main moves under an unchanged change and it rebases
cleanly, the next run only builds the workspace and checks formatting.

--full runs every stage, including the budgeted agreement and native campaigns, the release
profile, the full_gate_ tests and tools/check-native.sh; it reports every stage
and fails after the last (tools/main-full-run.sh).
"""

